Sure, I'd be happy to help! Promises in JavaScript are like "future values" that you can work with right now. Here's how they work:

Let's say you want to load some data from an API, but you don't know exactly when it will come back. You could write your code like this:

```js
function getData() {
  // make API request here...
}

// ...later on...
getData();
console.log("Data loaded!");
```

But what if the API takes a long time to respond? The `console.log()` statement will run before the data is actually available. That's where promises come in! Instead of immediately returning the data, we return a promise instead:

```js
function getData() {
  // make API request here...
  // return a new Promise object
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    // handle success or error cases
    if (success) {
      resolve(data);
    } else {
      reject(error);
    }
  });
}

